# ── Build artifacts ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bin/
*.exe
*.o
*.obj
*.elf
*.a
*.so
*.dll

# ── Binary data artifacts (datasets, not weights) ─────────────────────────────
# ANCHORING RULE (applied wholesale 2026-07-24, after the THIRD incident of this family). A pattern
# without a leading slash matches at ANY depth. So: anchor everything that names A PLACE (a specific
# directory of ours), leave free everything that names A TYPE OF ARTIFACT (bin/, logs/, __pycache__/) --
# anchoring those would start tracking build output the moment someone creates engine/bin/.
# The three incidents: `data/` swallowed the whole WS3 apparatus (15 files); `results/` unanchored removed
# the CONTRAST that would have made a missing [DECIDING] log obvious. Reactive fixing stopped being a
# strategy at three, so the remaining place-patterns are anchored here without waiting for a fourth.
data/*.bin
data/external/
/pg19/
telemetry_prose.csv
*.csv
/eureparl/
/kaggle/

# ── Generated outputs (logs, benchmark results) ───────────────────────────────
logs/
# Anchored, with a carve-out, because of the stage-3 incident (2026-07-24): every [DECIDING] number must
# live in a COMMITTED log and the brief must cite its path -- an adjudication is authority, not evidence.
# arm3's console log was never retained AND its natural home was invisible to git (kaggle_rung1/* plus this
# rule, unanchored), so nobody could notice the first fact. Every OTHER nested results/ dir is already
# covered by its own anchored rule (verified: phase56 outputs_run*, /data/, kaggle_mve/*, kaggle_rung1/*),
# so anchoring here costs no protection and buys a place decisions can be audited from.
results/*
!results/phase64_rung1/
/memoria_stato_attuale.md
/_transport/
/benchmarks_old/
docs/silicon_book/
# `test/` was the last live instance of the data/ hazard: unanchored, it swallowed a directory named
# `test` at any depth. Anchored it keeps hiding exactly what it hid before (the root test/ scratch tree,
# interactive_8m.py + interactive_generator.c) while no longer being able to eat a test suite someone
# adds under benchmarks/ or engine/.
/test/
# internal training dossiers (unvetted third-party PDFs; not for the public repo)
docs/research/training/

# ── Python environment and cache ──────────────────────────────────────────────
.venv/
.venv_wsl/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd
*.pyi
.pytest_cache/

# ── OS artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
ehthumbs.db

# ── Editor / IDE artifacts ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
*.swp
*~
*.bak
*.tmp
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
*.see
*.dec
*.enc
*.out.c
/graphify-out/

# ── Model checkpoints + experiment run outputs (verdicts live in memory/HANDOFF) ──
*.pt
benchmarks/phase56/outputs_run*/
benchmarks/phase56/output_*/

# ── Large data + weight blobs: kept on disk, never tracked (regeneratable) ──
# ANCHORED with a leading slash, and it must stay anchored. Un-anchored, `data/` matches a directory
# named `data` at ANY depth, which silently swallowed the whole of benchmarks/phase64/data/ -- the WS3
# apparatus. Gate-bearing code that git cannot see cannot be pushed before the run, and the
# pre-registration's falsifiability rests on those commit dates. The blobs live at the repo root; the
# per-package copies are covered by the kaggle_* rules below.
/data/
/weights/

# ── Local staging / scratch: never tracked ──
# Kaggle run-pack: per-account dataset copies (~2.6 GB each) + notebook cells, uploaded by hand.
# The README is the run procedure (documentation) and IS tracked; the payload never is.
# The pack carries a COPY of benchmarks/phase64/mve/*.py. That copy is a staging artifact and is
# NEVER the source of truth: fixes land in benchmarks/ (tracked) first, then get copied out here.
kaggle_mve/*
!kaggle_mve/README.md
# Same rule for the rung-1 run-packs (per-account arm packages + their tokenised data payload).
kaggle_rung1/*
!kaggle_rung1/README.md
# one-off local driver
/run_benchmarks_temp.py

# ── Internal-only docs (raw ideation / dev journals): kept private ──
docs/research/LLM_Reimagined_CPU_Native_Exploration_20260626/
